UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 33, Lesson 3: Symmetry and patterns', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Line symmetry means a shape can fold (or reflect) onto itself along a line; patterns can repeat with reflective symmetry.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nHow many lines of symmetry does a rectangle that is not a square have? A regular hexagon?\n\nFEEDBACK\n2; 6.\n\nWATCH FOR\nDrawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind lines of symmetry for twelve shapes and complete eight reflection patterns on grids.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Line symmetry means a shape can fold (or reflect) onto itself along a line; patterns can repeat with reflective symmetry.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Problem solving","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-2DS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find lines of symmetry for twelve shapes and complete eight reflection patterns on grids. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: 2; 6. Reasoning: I used symmetry by attending to line rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Drawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line. Correction: 2; 6. Why: the correction restores accurate use of symmetry.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Drawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses symmetry with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with symmetry, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"2; 6. This matches the lesson skill for symmetry. The correct choice applies the lesson skill; the others use a common mistake or ignore the evidence.","options":["2; 6.","Drawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work."],"question":"How many lines of symmetry does a rectangle that is not a square have? A regular hexagon?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Drawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Drawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line.","Line symmetry means a shape can fold (or reflect) onto itself along a line; patterns can repeat with reflective symmetry.","2; 6.","Careful use of line with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising symmetry?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named line, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the line used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"A learner is practising symmetry. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Drawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line.","Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about symmetry?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","A square has 4 lines of symmetry; an isosceles triangle often has 1; a scalene triangle has 0. Reflecting a pattern across a line creates…","Drawing a line that cuts equal area but does not match edges is not a symmetry line."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Line symmetry means a shape can fold (or reflect) onto itself along a line; patterns can repeat with… — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match.","Average the two answers without checking either.","Recheck places, operations and