od. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 33, Lesson 3: Symmetry and patterns","unit_number":9,"unit_title":"Angles, shapes and transformations","week":33,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA square has 4 lines of symmetry; an isosceles triangle often has 1; a scalene triangle has 0. Reflecting a pattern across a line creates a mirror copy.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Symmetry and patterns.","caption":"Equal area cut ≠ symmetry.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Line symmetry</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">fold match</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Pattern</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">mirror copy</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"True symmetry vs area-only cut.","caption":"Twelve + eight patterns.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\